n":"lower in AD","nucleus_padj":0.754737,"nucleus_log2fc":-0.101038,"nucleus_score":-0.680971,"pct_ad":0.0934066,"pct_control":0.101916,"donor_test":"not tested","donor_padj":null,"donor_log2fc":null,"donor_lfcse":null,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":null},{"cell_type":"Oli","gene_symbol":"RAD9A","gene_symbol_current":"RAD9A","gene_id":"ENSG00000172613","nucleus_tested":false,"nucleus_significant":null,"nucleus_direction":"not tested","nucleus_padj":null,"nucleus_log2fc":null,"nucleus_score":null,"pct_ad":null,"pct_control":null,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.935529,"donor_log2fc":-0.0713102,"donor_lfcse":0.135408,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":-0.00366439}],"modules":[{"cell_type":"Ex","gene_id":"ENSG00000172613","module":"blue","kme":0.236033,"hub":false,"module_direction":"lower in AD","module_padj":0.474279,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"In","gene_id":"ENSG00000172613","module":"yellow","kme":0.168386,"hub":false,"module_direction":"lower in AD","module_padj":0.41964,"module_significant":false}],"degas_bulk_de":false,"drug_target":false}},"description":[{"gene_id":"ENSG00000172613","entrez_id":["5883"],"uniprot":["Q99638"],"ncbi_summary":"This gene product is highly similar to Schizosaccharomyces pombe rad9, a cell cycle checkpoint protein required for cell cycle arrest and DNA damage repair. This protein possesses 3' to 5' exonuclease activity, which may contribute to its role in sensing and repairing DNA damage. It forms a checkpoint protein complex with RAD1 and HUS1. This complex is recruited by checkpoint protein RAD17 to the sites of DNA damage, which is thought to be important for triggering the checkpoint-signaling cascade. Alternatively spliced transcript variants encoding different isoforms have been found for this gene. [provided by RefSeq, Aug 2011].","ncbi_source":"NCBI Gene summary (RefSeq), via MyGene.info","uniprot_function":[{"accession":"Q99638","text":"Component of the 9-1-1 cell-cycle checkpoint response complex that plays a major role in DNA repair (PubMed:10713044, PubMed:17575048, PubMed:20545769, PubMed:21659603, PubMed:31135337). The 9-1-1 complex is recruited to DNA lesion upon damage by the RAD17-replication factor C (RFC) clamp loader complex (PubMed:21659603). Acts then as a sliding clamp platform on DNA for several proteins involved in long-patch base excision repair (LP-BER) (PubMed:21659603). The 9-1-1 complex stimulates DNA polymerase beta (POLB) activity by increasing its affinity for the 3'-OH end of the primer-template and stabilizes POLB to those sites where LP-BER proceeds; endonuclease FEN1 cleavage activity on substrates with double, nick, or gap flaps of distinct sequences and lengths; and DNA ligase I (LIG1) on long-patch base excision repair substrates (PubMed:21659603). The 9-1-1 complex is necessary for the recruitment of RHNO1 to sites of double-stranded breaks (DSB) occurring during the S phase (PubMed:21659603). RAD9A possesses 3'->5' double stranded DNA exonuclease activity (PubMed:10713044)."}],"uniprot_source":"UniProtKB/Swiss-Prot Function, release 2026_03","retrieved":"2026-10-21"}],"structures":[],"alphafold_accessions":[]}